<p>Last weekend was good. I came to a realization not too long ago that most of my weekends are good, which is a good thing in itself, but I wanted to ramble a little about why.</p>
<p>My sleeping issues (I should coin the term somehow. &ldquo;My Sleeping Issues&rdquo; sounds like a terrible album name stuck on repeat at this point) meant that I woke up at 4, walked around for a bit, and went back to bed around 6. This pattern has been going on since last Thursday, when I pushed myself to be in bed at around 23:00. Seems like I sleep 5-6 hours no matter what I do, so if I go to sleep earlier, I wake up earlier without enough sleep. The good part? Going to bed around 6 again means I&rsquo;m not a complete zombie the whole first half of the day, which definitely played a role here.</p>
<p>First off, there was a <em>lot</em> of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subnautica">Subnautica</a>. With <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1962700/Subnautica_2/">Part 2</a> out in early access, I saw <a href="https://www.humblebundle.com/store/subnautica-below-zero-switch">Subnautica: Below Zero was on sale on Humble Bundle</a> and snatched it. I never finished the first Subnautica (<a href="https://taonaw-blog.netlify.app/subnautica-and-fear/">it was too scary</a>, and yes, I need to get this post over to the new blog), and I wanted to try again. Man, Subnautica is a treat. It was easy to get hooked again. This time around, I watched plenty of YouTube videos to help me out with tips, and building my underwater base was fun in a relaxing kind of way. There&rsquo;s something about being able to organize a stressful situation that doesn&rsquo;t let you breathe (literally in this game) into something safe that makes sense to you. And, yes, <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/05/17/of-overworking-and-delegating.html">that&rsquo;s also a metaphor</a>.</p>
<p>I <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/04/23/things-i-want-to-do.html">mentioned before I want to make some video content for gaming</a>, but it&rsquo;s not easy. Or rather, it&rsquo;s a <em>lot</em>. I am no stranger to OBS Studio, but I had to re-learn to do a couple of things, and even then, going back and watching an hour or more of content and starting editing takes a lot of time. It&rsquo;s a serious commitment. I haven&rsquo;t given up on the idea, but I might change it around a bit toward a mix of writing and video content. I&rsquo;m not sure yet. There are too many good games out there, and I don&rsquo;t think there are enough places reviewing them from a personal perspective (by the way, let me introduce you to <a href="https://blog.lauramichet.com">Laura Michet&rsquo;s blog</a>. She is a game writer/narrative designer, and she writes often).</p>
<p>Then I had a nice hangout with my partner, who works as an EMT. They are awesome in general (obviously I think so), and they recently got themselves a car, and driving in Manhattan is&hellip; well, let&rsquo;s just say parking is one tough SOB boss. Imagine working a 9-hour shift in an ambulance, driving around (on a weekend, because during the week they&rsquo;re busy with their other job), just to drive another two hours, the last hour and a half(!) of it is in order to find parking. They pretty much showed up just to crash and fall asleep, but then the next day, we went to the Farmer&rsquo;s market together and drove around and walked the parks of upper Manhattan. It&rsquo;s a sort of buffer, a break that makes my brain slow down a bit and appreciate my life outside of work and tech.</p>
<p>Which means that when I got back to my /own/ tech on Sunday, I was refreshed and full of energy. This weekend I <a href="https://taonaw.com/2026/05/31/installing-harper-on-kubuntu-the.html">installed Harper on my Linux desktop</a>. Working through it but especially writing the post about it requires geek brain energy, which I just don&rsquo;t have during the week. I really enjoy these projects; I learn more about stuff I&rsquo;m passionate about, and it&rsquo;s very rewarding.</p>
<p>I also wrote a lot in my journal, which I&rsquo;m reorganizing, and it made me realize that, hey, I write a <em>lot</em>. Between my posts, emails to other bloggers, journaling, and experimenting with TiddlyWiki, there&rsquo;s a lot of keyboard typing going on. This, as usual, makes me appreciate Emacs and org-mode, which has its own dedicated category. But that&rsquo;s what happens when you can customize everything about a tool you use all the time: it&rsquo;s a game in itself.</p>
<p>And now, with coffee done and this draft more or less complete, it&rsquo;s time to get in the shower and quickly get some groceries before the work day gets the chance to kick my ass back into gear.</p>
